-- title:
--   Degree-zero divisors generated by differences of two points
-- statement:
--   Let $K$ and $F$ be fields with $F$ a $K$-algebra, with $K$ algebraically closed and with `IsCurveOver K F`, i.e. $F/K$ satisfies: every nonzero $f \in F$ has a divisor whose coefficient at each place is $\mathrm{ord}_v(f)$ and whose degree is $0$; the residue field of every place is a finite $K$-module; and $\Omega[F/K]$ is a free $F$-module of rank one. Here a place of $F/K$ is a valuation subring of $F$ which contains the image of $K$, is not all of $F$, and is a principal ideal ring; a divisor is a finitely supported function from places to $\mathbb{Z}$, and its degree is $\sum_v D(v)\,[\kappa(v):K]$, so that `Divisor.degZero` is the subgroup of divisors of degree zero. Let $X$ be any type and $\mathrm{pt} : X \to \mathrm{Place}\,K\,F$ a surjective map. The assertion is that `Divisor.degZero` is contained in the additive subgroup of divisors generated by the set of divisors of the shape $[\mathrm{pt}(a)] - [\mathrm{pt}(b)]$ with $a, b \in X$, where $[v]$ denotes the divisor $\mathrm{single}\ v\ 1$.
--
--   This is the standard presentation of the degree-zero part of the divisor group of a curve over an algebraically closed constant field by differences of two rational points, phrased with an arbitrary surjective parametrisation $\mathrm{pt}$ of the set of places so that callers whose points are given as orbits or as vertices of a combinatorial datum can apply it directly. It is used in the treatment of the Čerednik–Drinfel'd uniformisation, where statements about $\mathrm{Pic}^0$ and its pullback/pushforward maps along a finite morphism are reduced to the case of a difference of two points.
-- source:
--   https://github.com/anthropics/fermats-last-theorem/blob/aa2d8b34692b16c70f699536de0d8e75b9a3e9ef/Theorems/Thm_AlgebraicCurve_Divisor_degZero_le_closure_single_sub_single_of_surjective.lean

